food_ingredients:collard-greens a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:altLabel "collard"@en ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:leaf-vegetable ;
    skos:definition "Collards (collard greens) are a leafy green vegetable in the cabbage family — the same species as kale, cabbage, and broccoli (Brassica oleracea), just bred for large, loose, dark green leaves rather than a tight head. Earthy, slightly bitter, and hearty — milder than mustard greens, tougher and more assertive than spinach. Traditionally slow-cooked (often with smoked meat, onion, garlic, vinegar, and pepper flakes in Southern U.S. cooking) until tender — but they can also be sautéed, steamed, or even eaten raw/massaged in salads if sliced thin. It's the same species as cabbage (Brassica oleracea), but not a cabbage in the way we normally mean the word."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "collard greens"@en ;
    off:species "Brassica oleracea"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "11161"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Collards, raw"@en ;
