food_ingredients:dock a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:leaf-vegetable ;
    skos:definition "Dock (genus Rumex, especially curly dock — Rumex crispus — and broad-leaved dock — Rumex obtusifolius) is a wild edible plant. Young leaves have a tart, lemony, slightly sour flavor thanks to oxalic acid — similar to sorrel but a bit milder. As leaves mature they get tougher and more bitter/astringent. Young leaves: eaten raw in salads, or added to soups, stews, and sautés like spinach.; Older leaves: usually cooked (boiled or braised) to mellow the sourness and soften the texture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "dock"@en,
        "Rumex"@la ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "11616"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Dock, raw"@en .

