food_ingredients:drumstick-leaves a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:leaf-vegetable ;
    skos:definition "Small, oval-shaped leaflets of the moringa tree (Moringa oleifera), sometimes called the horseradish tree or ben tree. The leaves grow in clusters on the branches, often described as feathery or fern-like in arrangement. They're a popular vegetable in South Asian, Southeast Asian, and African cuisines. Added to salads, or cooked briefly like spinach — sautéed, added to soups, dals, or stir-fries."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "drumstick leaves"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "11222"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Drumstick leaves, raw"@en .

food_ingredients:drumstick-pods a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:fruit-vegetable ;
    skos:definition "\"Drumstick\" is the common name for the long, slender seed pods of the moringa tree (Moringa oleifera), sometimes called the horseradish tree or ben tree. They're a popular vegetable in South Asian, Southeast Asian, and African cuisines. Long, thin, ridged green pods, often 1–3 feet long. Mild, slightly sweet, a bit like asparagus or green beans."@en ;
