food_ingredients:epazote a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:herb ;
    skos:definition "Epazote (Dysphania ambrosioides, formerly Chenopodium ambrosioides) is a pungent, aromatic herb widely used in Mexican and Central American cooking. The flavor is hard to pin down — people describe it as a mix of citrus, mint, anise, tar, and even petroleum-like notes. It's strong, so it's used in moderation, usually just a sprig or a few leaves per dish, added toward the end of cooking to preserve its aroma. Epazote contains compounds (like ascaridole) that can be toxic in concentrated amounts, such as in essential oil form. Using fresh or dried leaves in normal cooking quantities is considered safe and is how it's traditionally consumed — but it shouldn't be consumed as a concentrated extract or in medicinal \"high dose\" preparations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "epazote"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "11984"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Epazote, raw"@en .

