food_ingredients:fiddlehead-fern a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:leaf-vegetable ;
    skos:definition "Fiddlehead ferns are the tightly coiled, immature fronds of certain fern species, harvested in early spring before they unfurl. They're named for their resemblance to the curled end (scroll) of a fiddle or violin. Most common edible variety: Ostrich fern (Matteuccia struthiopteris) is the safest and most widely eaten species. Not all fern fiddleheads are edible — some are toxic, so identification matters. Often described as a cross between asparagus, green beans, and a slightly nutty, grassy taste. Fiddleheads must be cooked thoroughly (boiled or steamed for several minutes, then sautéed if desired) before eating. Raw or undercooked fiddleheads have caused foodborne illness outbreaks — they can carry bacteria or contain compounds that cause nausea, cramps, and diarrhea if not properly prepared."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "fiddlehead fern"@en,
        "Matteuccia struthiopteris"@la ;
    off:usdaNdb2Code "11996"@en ;
    off:usdaNdb2Name "Fiddlehead ferns, frozen, unprepared"@en ;
    off:usdaNdb2Process "en:frozen, en:unprepared"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "11995"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Fiddlehead ferns, raw"@en .

