food_ingredients:firewood-leaf a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:altLabel "firewoord leaves"@en,
        "Epilobium angustifolium"@la ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:leaf-vegetable ;
    skos:definition "Fireweed (Chamaenerion angustifolium, also called Epilobium angustifolium) is a tall wildflower with bright pink-purple flower spikes, common in northern temperate regions. Young leaves, picked before the plant flowers, can be eaten raw in salads or cooked like spinach. As the plant matures, the leaves get tougher and more bitter, so timing matters. Fireweed has a mild laxative effect in some people if eaten in quantity, so it's usually eaten in modest amounts rather than as a main dish."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "firewood leaf"@en,
        "Chamaenerion angustifolium"@la ;
