food_ingredients:fuki a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:vegetable ;
    skos:definition "Fuki (Petasites japonicus) is a flowering plant native to Europe and parts of Asia and North America. In Japanese cuisine, fuki is a well-established vegetable and has been eaten for centuries: The leaf stalks (petioles) are the main edible part — boiled, then peeled and used in simmered dishes (nimono), stir-fries, or as a side dish; The flower buds (fuki no tō) are a beloved spring delicacy, eaten tempura-fried or as a miso paste — they have a pleasant, slightly bitter taste."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "fuki"@en,
        "Petasites japonicus"@la ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "11106"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Butterbur, (fuki), raw"@en .

