food_ingredients:hickorynut a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:nut ;
    skos:definition "A hickory nut is the edible nut produced by trees in the genus Carya — the same genus that includes pecans. They're native mostly to North America, with a few species in Asia. Encased in a thick, husk-like outer covering that splits open (fully or partially, depending on species) when ripe, revealing a hard shell. Inside that shell is the actual nutmeat. Flavor varies a lot by species — some, like shagbark hickory, are sweet and rich (similar to a pecan, which makes sense given the family connection), while others, like pignut hickory, can be bitter or astringent."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "hickorynut"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "12130"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Nuts, hickorynuts, dried"@en .

