food_ingredients:maitake-mushroom a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:mushroom ;
    skos:definition "Maitake mushrooms (Grifola frondosa) are a type of edible mushroom native to Japan, China, and North America, where they grow wild at the base of oak, maple, and elm trees. Earthy, rich, and savory, often described as woodsy with a slightly peppery note. They have a nice meaty texture when cooked, similar to other polypore mushrooms. Popular in Japanese and other Asian cuisines — sautéed, roasted, added to soups, stir-fries, or used in broths. Their frilly texture crisps up nicely when roasted or pan-fried."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "maitake mushroom"@en,
        "Grifola frondosa"@la ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "11993"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Mushrooms, maitake, raw"@en .

