food_ingredients:mustard-greens a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:leaf-vegetable ;
    skos:definition "Mustard greens are the leafy tops of the mustard plant (Brassica juncea), and they're a true member of the mustard family — grown specifically for their peppery, pungent leaves rather than for seeds or oil (though the same plant family produces mustard seeds too). Mustard greens have a distinctly sharp, spicy, peppery bite — similar to the kick you get from horseradish, wasabi, or arugula, but more pronounced. Cooking mellows the heat somewhat, but they still have more punch than almost any other leafy green."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "mustard greens"@en ;
    off:comment "the latin name is also associated with mustard cabbage."@en ;
    off:usdaNdb2Code "11272"@en ;
    off:usdaNdb2Name "Mustard greens, frozen, unprepared"@en ;
    off:usdaNdb2Process "en:frozen, en:unprepared"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "11270"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Mustard greens, raw"@en .

