food_ingredients:new-zealand-spinach a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:leaf-vegetable ;
    skos:definition "New Zealand spinach (Tetragonia tetragonioides) isn't a true spinach at all. it's eaten much the same way as regular spinach — the young leaves and shoot tips are harvested and used cooked (steamed, sautéed, boiled, or added to soups and stir-fries). It's usually not eaten raw in large quantities because of its oxalate content, and cooking also helps soften its slightly thicker, fuzzier texture."@en ;
