food_ingredients:pale-brown-amaranth-seed a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:amaranth ;
    skos:definition "Amaranth seed, pale brown (Amaranthus cruentus), known regionally as Rajagaro (Gujarati), Ramdana (Hindi), and Cavali biya (Marathi), among other local names. Unlike the deep black-seeded variety, this pale brown form has a milder, subtly sweet, nutty flavor. In Indian cuisine, it's commonly popped and used in fasting foods (vrat ka khana), ground into flour for flatbreads, or mixed with jaggery to make chikki-style sweets. Nutritionally, it's a good source of plant protein, dietary fiber, iron, and calcium, and is naturally gluten-free—making it a popular grain substitute during religious fasting periods across India."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "pale brown amaranth seed"@en ;
    off:ifctFoodCode "A002"@en ;
    off:ifctFoodName "Amaranth seed, pale brown"@en .

