food_ingredients:sesbania-flower a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:plant ;
    skos:definition "The flowers come from Sesbania grandiflora, a fast-growing tropical tree also called the vegetable hummingbird, hummingbird tree, or agati. It's a popular edible flower across South and Southeast Asian cuisines. Before cooking, the bitter stamens and pistil inside each flower are usually pinched out, leaving the sweeter petals. They taste mildly sweet and slightly bitter, with a delicate, slightly crunchy texture once cooked."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "sesbania flower"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "11447"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Sesbania flower, raw"@en .

