food_ingredients:sugar-apple a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:altLabel "sweetsop"@en ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:fruit ;
    skos:definition "A sugar apple (also called sweetsop) is a tropical fruit from the Annona squamosa tree, native to the tropical Americas and West Indies. It has a bumpy, knobby green skin made up of overlapping segments that look almost like scales or a pinecone. When ripe, the segments separate easily. The white, creamy flesh inside is sweet and custard-like — hence \"sugar\" apple. The taste is often described as a blend of vanilla, pear, and banana, with a grainy, melt-in-your-mouth texture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "sugar apple"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "9321"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Sugar-apples, (sweetsop), raw"@en .

