food_ingredients:white-flowered-gourd a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:altLabel "bottle gourd"@en,
        "calabash gourd"@en,
        "long melon"@en,
        "opo squash"@en ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:fruit-vegetable ;
    skos:definition "\"White-flowered gourd\" (calabash) refers to Lagenaria siceraria — also known as bottle gourd, calabash gourd, opo squash, or long melon. It's the same plant whose hard-shelled fruits are dried and used as bottles, bowls, or musical instruments, but when eaten as food it's harvested young and tender, before the shell hardens. Very mild, slightly sweet, with a texture similar to zucchini or chayote — it takes on the flavor of whatever it's cooked with. Common in cuisines: South Asian (India, Pakistan, Bangladesh — called lauki, doodhi, or ghiya), Chinese (hu lu), Vietnamese, Italian (cucuzza), and West African cooking. One caution worth knowing: bottle gourds can occasionally develop high levels of cucurbitacins (the compound that makes some gourds bitter), which can cause food poisoning. The rule of thumb is — if it tastes bitter, don't eat it, even if just one bite."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "white-flowered gourd"@en,
        "Lagenaria siceraria"@la ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "11218"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Gourd, white-flowered (calabash), raw"@en .

