    skos:broader food_ingredients:mushroom ;
    skos:definition "\"Cloud ear\" (also called wood ear, black fungus, or mù'ěr 木耳 in Chinese) refers to mushrooms in the genus Auricularia — most commonly Auricularia auricula-judae or Auricularia cornea. They're a type of jelly fungus that grows on decaying or living wood, especially elder, beech, and other broadleaf trees. Cloud ears are prized more for their texture than flavor — they're crunchy, slightly gelatinous, and almost cartilaginous when cooked, but have very little taste of their own. That makes them great at soaking up other flavors in a dish."@en ;
