food_ingredients:monkfish a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:altLabel "Angler"@de,
        "Lophius"@de,
        "Lotte"@de,
        "angler fish"@en,
        "anglerfish"@en,
        "rape"@es ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:saltwater-fish,
        food_ingredients:white-fish ;
    skos:definition "\"Anglerfish\" is a broad term for an order of fish (Lophiiformes) known for using a fleshy growth on their head like a fishing lure to attract prey. The fish sold as \"monkfish\" in seafood markets typically refers to species in the genus Lophius — like the American anglerfish (Lophius americanus) or European anglerfish (Lophius piscatorius) — which live on the seafloor in relatively shallow coastal waters. Monkfish is prized in cooking mostly for its tail meat, which is the part typically sold and eaten. Texture and flavor: It has a firm, dense, meaty texture — often compared to lobster — with a mild, slightly sweet flavor. This has earned it the nickname \"poor man's lobster.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "عفريت البحر"@ar,
