food_ingredients:cottonseed a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:altLabel "cotton seed"@en ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:seed ;
    skos:definition "Cottonseed is the seed of the cotton plant (genus Gossypium), harvested as a byproduct of cotton fiber production. After cotton bolls are picked, the fluffy fiber (lint) is separated from the seeds during a process called ginning. Each cotton boll contains several seeds embedded in the fiber. Raw cottonseed contains a natural compound called gossypol, which is toxic to humans in significant quantities. Some specialty \"glandless\" cotton varieties have been bred to have naturally low gossypol levels, and these are sometimes explored as a protein source (the seed kernel is fairly protein-rich), but this isn't mainstream or widely available commercially."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "Baumwollsaat"@de,
        "cottonseed"@en,
        "semillas de algodón"@es,
        "graine de coton"@fr,
        "pamuk"@hr,
        "semi di cotone"@it,
        "katoenzaad"@nl .

