food_ingredients:tilefish a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:saltwater-fish ;
    skos:definition "Tilefish is a type of saltwater fish found mainly in the western Atlantic Ocean, from the northeastern U.S. down through the Gulf of Mexico. There are a few species, but the most commonly eaten ones are golden tilefish (also called \"white tilefish\" in markets) and blueline tilefish. Flavor and texture: Mild, sweet flavor with firm, moist flesh — often compared to a cross between lobster and crab in taste, which is why it's sometimes nicknamed \"poor man's lobster.\" The texture holds up well to various cooking methods. Cooking methods: It works well baked, roasted, pan-seared, grilled, or poached. The skin can be crisped up nicely in a pan. It's popular in Japanese cuisine too, where it's called amadai and often prepared with the scales left on and fried until crispy."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "tilefish"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbCode "15112"@en ;
    off:usdaNdbName "Fish, tilefish, raw"@en .

