food_ingredients:winged-beans a off:FoodIngredient ;
    skos:broader food_ingredients:vegetable ;
    skos:definition "Winged beans (Psophocarpus tetragonolobus) are a tropical legume known for their distinctive four-sided, ruffled or \"winged\" pods — the edges look like jagged ribbons running down the length of each pod. Almost the entire plant is edible: pods, seeds, leaves, flowers, and even the tuberous roots can all be eaten. That's unusual among legumes. High in protein: the seeds in particular are very protein- and nutrient-dense, which is part of why they've been studied as a potential \"supercrop\" for tropical regions. Native to Southeast Asia and New Guinea, and widely grown across South and Southeast Asia, as well as parts of Africa."@en ;
    skos:inScheme off:food_ingredients ;
    skos:prefLabel "winged beans"@en,
        "Psophocarpus tetragonolobus"@la ;
    off:comment "what would be the usda proxy? How is it mainly used?"@en .

